@1 = ~Free...~
@2 = ~You... are free. Yes.~
@3 = ~Another gladiator forced into the arena?~
@4 = ~I cannot let you live, vile creature.~
@5 = ~You freed me. Now I can focus on my mission. Step aside.~
@6 = ~Stay out of my way.~
@7 = ~I have nothing more to say without Viconia here.~
@8 = ~Who is it!~
@9 = ~V... Valas?~
@10 = ~Vic... I have no family. House De'Vir is finished, and so am I.~
@11 = ~I... I survived. I thought I would never see you again. You are... here, in the flesh. So far from Menzoberranzan.~
@12 = ~I am here on an essential mission in Lolth's name. I have no business with anyone outside it.~
@13 = ~Valas... you do not remember. It... it is not you. Not anymore.~
@14 = ~You must leave before I have to... I cannot allow Lolth's will to be endangered by outsiders. My mission is sacred... my mission... I... I will not do this...~
@15 = ~I deceived myself into believing you were dead. This fate is worse than death. Lolth be damned for making you into this... unholy abomination. It should never have happened.~
@16 = ~Y-you need to leave. Now.~
@17 = ~Viconia...~
@18 = ~We must leave. You cannot reason with him.~
@19 = ~Valas?~
@20 = ~We are only moments away from whatever the haszak is planning. If we... end up fighting him, I... may not be able to raise my weapon against him.~
@21 = ~This is all remarkably devastating, but can we leave this rathole?! Need I remind you we are about to be devoured by mind flayers?! (If the great Edwin Odesseiron is to die here through the incompetence of these simpletons, I swear I shall be livid in the afterlife.)~
@22 = ~I may have little affection for you, Viconia, but I do feel your loss. In truth, you and I have both lost someone, though the fate of your brother is tragic beyond belief.~
@23 = ~To be stripped of humanity... if drow can even be said to have it... and become this abomination? M-my skin crawls at the thought of so gruesome a fate.~
@24 = ~A beast born of evil and darkness. I long to see sunlight again. We have spent far too much time in this underworld.~
@25 = ~By Torm, this atrocity is beyond foul. That drow society and its goddess would inflict such horrors upon their own people beggars belief.~
@26 = ~This creature is a stain upon the natural world. Such a horrific distortion exceeds my understanding. Lolth's vengeance is grave indeed.~
@27 = ~By Baervan's golden turnip-flail, look at you! Your... multifaceted physique reminds me of my second cousin twice removed, Pipwig. He believed the secret to faster turnip harvesting lay in the common cave spider. He spent three summers stitching six extra pairs of woollen trousers onto a giant huntsman, while feeding it mashed rutabagas and blackberry brandy. It ended badly, of course. The spider developed a terrible rash, a taste for fine tailoring, and ran off with Aunt Gretchen's finest lace petticoats.~
@28 = ~No more words! Your stories hold no interest for me, nor do I need your opinions on matters beyond your understanding.~
@29 = ~The fate of drow who defy their society is cruel. Much as I despise what Viconia stands for, I cannot help but pity her. I knew your past was painful, but never realised you carried such anger within you. Had I known the whole story, perhaps I would have judged you less harshly.~
@30 = ~For the sake of clarity, this changes nothing between us. We have our differences, and so they shall remain, halfling.~
@31 = ~I would have it no other way, drow.~
@32 = ~Looking at him makes me deeply uneasy. Magic is perilous enough, but this grotesque magical curse is... repulsive.~
@33 = ~Nothing more than a spider. I would crush it beneath my boot, if you allowed me.~
@34 = ~Threaten my brother again, and I will do the crushing. If anyone is to end his torment, I will permit no one but myself.~
@35 = ~Bah! The wretched abomination looks like a troll's backside! Cleavin' skulls is one thing, but bein' doomed to crawl about as a half-arsed spider-man is a fate too foul even for a drow!~
@36 = ~I cannot help but feel a kinship with this creature. He and I both endured unnatural ordeals that turned us into grotesque remnants of what we once were. It is almost... tragic.~
@37 = ~A drider: the ultimate, tragic image of light smothered and twisted by Lolth's darkness. No warmth or compassion I offer could soothe your pain, Viconia, but despite our differences, know that I mourn your loss.~
@38 = ~Silence, monk! No follower of Selûne will offer me condolences. Hold your tongue, lest I take it!~
@39 = ~Viconia, I am so sorry. I... never even knew you had a brother. I cannot imagine what you must feel, seeing him like this. You and I are hardly friends, but I wish things were different. You seem to care for him... to worry. I never expected a drow to show such feelings.~
@40 = ~A cheerful brat like you could never understand loss. And you are right about friendship: it is for the weak. I need no meddling from you or your relentlessly sunny nature.~
@41 = ~Viconia, I may not have lost someone to a curse like this, but my own father was a monster. Bhaal himself, as vile and oppressive as Lolth. What happened to your brother is awful. I know no words can undo it, but if I could do anything to ease your pain... I would.~
@42 = ~Speeches such as this bring me no comfort, child. We... we should leave him be.~
@43 = ~You are right. We cannot afford to remain here.~
@44 = ~This is all remarkably devastating, but can we leave this rathole?! Need I remind you we are about to be devoured by mind flayers?! (If the great Edwin Odesseiron is to die here through the incompetence of these simpletons, I swear I shall be livid in the afterlife.)~
@45 = ~I may have little affection for you, Viconia, but I do feel your loss. In truth, you and I have both lost someone, though the fate of your brother is tragic beyond belief.~
@46 = ~To be stripped of humanity... if drow can even be said to have it... and become this abomination? M-my skin crawls at the thought of so gruesome a fate.~
@47 = ~A beast born of evil and darkness. I long to see sunlight again. We have spent far too much time in this underworld.~
@48 = ~By Torm, this atrocity is beyond foul. That drow society and its goddess would inflict such horrors upon their own people beggars belief.~
@49 = ~This creature is a stain upon the natural world. Such a horrific distortion exceeds my understanding. Lolth's vengeance is grave indeed.~
@50 = ~By Baervan's golden turnip-flail, look at you! Your... multifaceted physique reminds me of my second cousin twice removed, Pipwig. He believed the secret to faster turnip harvesting lay in the common cave spider. He spent three summers stitching six extra pairs of woollen trousers onto a giant huntsman, while feeding it mashed rutabagas and blackberry brandy. It ended badly, of course. The spider developed a terrible rash, a taste for fine tailoring, and ran off with Aunt Gretchen's finest lace petticoats.~
@51 = ~No more words! Your stories hold no interest for me, nor do I need your opinions on matters beyond your understanding.~
@52 = ~The fate of drow who defy their society is cruel. Much as I despise what Viconia stands for, I cannot help but pity her. I knew your past was painful, but never realised you carried such anger within you. Had I known the whole story, perhaps I would have judged you less harshly.~
@53 = ~For the sake of clarity, this changes nothing between us. We have our differences, and so they shall remain, halfling.~
@54 = ~I would have it no other way, drow.~
@55 = ~Looking at him makes me deeply uneasy. Magic is perilous enough, but this grotesque magical curse is... repulsive.~
@56 = ~Nothing more than a spider. I would crush it beneath my boot, if you allowed me.~
@57 = ~Threaten my brother again, and I will do the crushing. If anyone is to end his torment, I will permit no one but myself.~
@58 = ~Bah! The wretched abomination looks like a troll's backside! Cleavin' skulls is one thing, but bein' doomed to crawl about as a half-arsed spider-man is a fate too foul even for a drow!~
@59 = ~I cannot help but feel a kinship with this creature. He and I both endured unnatural ordeals that turned us into grotesque remnants of what we once were. It is almost... tragic.~
@60 = ~A drider: the ultimate, tragic image of light smothered and twisted by Lolth's darkness. No warmth or compassion I offer could soothe your pain, Viconia, but despite our differences, know that I mourn your loss.~
@61 = ~Silence, monk! No follower of Selûne will offer me condolences. Hold your tongue, lest I take it!~
@62 = ~Viconia, I am so sorry. I... never even knew you had a brother. I cannot imagine what you must feel, seeing him like this. You and I are hardly friends, but I wish things were different. You seem to care for him... to worry. I never expected a drow to show such feelings.~
@63 = ~A cheerful brat like you could never understand loss. And you are right about friendship: it is for the weak. I need no meddling from you or your relentlessly sunny nature.~
@64 = ~Viconia, I may not have lost someone to a curse like this, but my own father was a monster. Bhaal himself, as vile and oppressive as Lolth. What happened to your brother is awful. I know no words can undo it, but if I could do anything to ease your pain... I would.~
@65 = ~Speeches such as this bring me no comfort, child. We... we should leave him be.~
@66 = ~No! No more words! We will not discuss this. He was captured... just as we were. I... we cannot help him. We need to get out of here. He is an abomination, cursed by Lolth herself. There is nothing I can do.~
@67 = ~M... may Shar look upon you with favour. Valas... I am sorry for what happened to you. I wish things could have been different.~
@68 = ~No! Do not waste words on him! He is... not himself. He is now p... part of my past. There is nothing I can do to save him from this fate. M... may Shar look upon you with favour. Valas. I wish things could have been different.~
@69 = ~Calon jhal ol' natha, Valas. Your... cage is open. You are free. Do whatever you see fit... brother.~
@70 = ~For so long I thought my brother was merely a mindless creature, trapped in the body of a half-spider and stripped of every tie to his past. I was wrong. Thankfully, I was wrong. We should not waste time. We have a mission to finish, and I would rather not be ambushed again.~
@71 = ~Agreed. Just... let me collect myself for a moment.~
@72 = ~I cannot speak with you.~
@73 = ~Do you need... a moment?~
@74 = ~Touching as this is, we are prisoners, and time is of the essence.~
@75 = ~(Without another glance, the drider moves past you and through the nearest doorway. Whatever task drives him, he seems determined to fulfil it, heedless of what has just happened.)~
@76 = ~You... made it out. Unharmed, I trust.~
@77 = ~Valas?~
@78 = ~Lolth demanded a particular sacrifice, but I cannot fulfil her command. I will return in disgrace, and she will destroy me for failing her.~
@79 = ~Valas, I...~
@80 = ~Do not. Living in this hideous form is torment. If only Lolth could wipe me from existence altogether. If only we could go back to the days of our childhood.~
@81 = ~You... remember.~
@82 = ~Yes. I remember. Our bond was frowned upon because we were unlike any other family. Our refusal to embrace drow customs kept us apart from our kin, but we were happy. You shielded me from our matron's wrath, and I did what I could to make you feel safe. I remember making you smile... even laugh. And...~
@83 = ~I remember the last time we saw each other. Our enraged mother... she tried to kill you, and...~
@84 = ~...and you killed her. You saved my life, and I could never repay you. I never meant for you to be turned into this monstrosity! Every time I looked back, I hoped to see you behind me, so we could escape together. But you were never there. I dreamed of you each night, banished and punished for helping me flee.~
@85 = ~I fought each day not to let you down, praying to Shar that you were watching over me. To see you like... this. You and I deserved a second chance.~
@86 = ~It was not your fault! I chose to save you. I was glad you escaped, but Lolth... she forced me to serve her and to search for you.~
@87 = ~She sensed that you had returned. She sent me here to find you and deliver you to her, but... I cannot! I will not!~
@88 = ~Our culture, our mother... Lolth! They shaped us into cruel, obedient servants, when all we wanted was the freedom to choose. Every time we defied Lolth, we suffered for it.~
@89 = ~Viconia, we cannot change the past, but we can decide what happens now. I was ordered to kill you. Yet if I defect, perhaps I could begin again. Forsaking Lolth once seemed impossible, but seeing you has given me the courage to resist her.~
@90 = ~Where would you go? I owe much to those with whom I travel, and I have sworn to remain at their side. But taking you with us... would be dangerous. Surface folk would see your form and attack on sight.~
@91 = ~I have heard of an encampment devoted to Eilistraee, the Dark Maiden, somewhere on the surface. Perhaps I could reach it unseen. They offer shelter and guidance to those fleeing Lolth's tyranny. They might even accept a drider. I wish you could come, but I see you have placed your trust in your companions.~
@92 = ~And you. If my sister trusts you, I shall do the same. Take care of her. If harm comes to her, I will find you, even if I must cross all Toril.~
@93 = ~Aluve', dalniluk. May Shar guide your path until we meet again.~
@94 = ~Alur Ol Dhas. Take care of yourself and... farewell, sister. I will be waiting.~
@95 = ~Farewell, Valas.~
@96 = ~Viconia, are you all right?~
@97 = ~Enough family drama. We need to get out of here.~
